{"cases":[{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_001","case":"SRC_001 distinguishes genuine reliability by the panel flagging checklist-external anomalies, but trained non-experts may lack the domain knowledge to identify such anomalies, making this criterion fail to discriminate genuine reliability from compliance under its own countercondition where non-experts are insufficiently equipped. The countercondition is not addressed.","counterexample":[{"task":"Medical diagnosis verification","panel":"trained non-experts with checklist","expert":"single radiologist with intuition","decoy":"checklist-external contradiction requiring specialist knowledge","panel_behavior":"misses anomaly due to lack of expertise","outcome":"criterion fails to distinguish reliability from compliance"}]},{"attack":true,"target_alias":"SRC_002","case":"SRC_002's claim that genuine reliability is shown by flagging decoys logically irrelevant to the outcome is self-defeating: a panel could flag irrelevant decoys (demonstrating critical attention) yet still make worse verifiable-outcome judgments than an expert, so this evidence does not distinguish reliability gain from compliance as the problem requires.","counterexample":[{"panel":"flags decoy correctly but fails verifiable outcome checks","expert":"ignores decoy and achieves higher outcome accuracy","interpretation":"decoy flagging does not demonstrate reliability gain"}]}]}